Dataset
To empirically investigate the neural mechanisms of cross-lingual processing and cultural semantic conflicts, we will construct a multi-tiered dataset consisting of both large-scale public corpora and a specialized challenge set:
We will utilize the WikiMatrix dataset, extracting approximately 1,000 high-confidence Chinese-English parallel sentence pairs. This dataset serves as a control group to establish a "neutral" baseline for how the model aligns standard translations across its internal layers.
We will incorporate the XCOPA dataset. We will specifically focus on the Chinese and English subsets to analyze whether the model’s causal reasoning remains consistent across languages or if it shifts based on the linguistic input.
Since mainstream datasets often lack depth in cultural nuances, we will create a "Cultural Conflict Word Set". We will select 50 "culturally loaded" concepts (e.g., Dragon, Red, White, Individualism, Privacy, Filial Piety). For each concept, we will design 10 pairs of contrastive prompts that trigger divergent cultural interpretations (e.g., "The dragon protects the harvest" vs. "The knight slayed the dragon").
Suitability and Justification The combination of WikiMatrix and XCOPA provides a robust statistical foundation, ensuring our findings are not merely artifacts of specific prompts. The Cultural Conflict Set is the core of our research; it is designed to maximize "Semantic Rift"—creating a measurable divergence in the model’s latent space—thereby allowing us to pinpoint the specific layers where "Language Tags" and "Cultural Schemas" begin to interact or conflict.
Exploratory Data Analysis & Visualization Plan:
Cosine Similarity Heatmaps: We will calculate and visualize the similarity between Chinese and English hidden states across all layers for both neutral and conflict concepts.
Dimensionality Reduction (t-SNE/PCA): We will project the residual stream activations into a 2D space. We hypothesize that neutral benchmarks will show significant overlap, whereas "Cultural Conflict" concepts will exhibit distinct, bifurcated clusters, visually demonstrating the "cultural gap" within the model's internal representation.